# Full run, group g1, batch 09. Written from the fact sheets, following scripts/GUIDE.md.

我:
  object: a hand on the left holding a long-shafted weapon, a halberd, on the right
  history: >-
    An ideogram: a hand holding a halberd 戈. The oldest forms drew the weapon alone, with its blade
    on the shaft.
  layout: >-
    The open hand, palm toward the viewer with the wrist below, fills the left half and grips the
    halberd. The halberd fills the right two thirds of the square and crosses behind the hand: a
    pale wooden shaft across, a long curved pole down the right side, a pale grey blade at its side,
    and a small pale red tassel at the top. The weapon stands in a plain pose and is not shown being
    used. Colours are pale.
  exact:
    - The hand's two short strokes (the fingertips at the top and the line across the lower part) do not cross the weapon's strokes except where the shaft passes the hand.
    - The long line across (the shaft) crosses the hand's vertical and also crosses the long curved pole.
    - The hand's vertical ends in a small hook up to the left and does not touch the lower sweeping stroke of the weapon; a small gap lies between them.
    - The fingertip stroke at the top left stops short of the long curved pole; they do not touch.
    - The long curved pole runs down, bends to the right near the bottom and ends in a small hook up.
    - The small dot at the top right stands free of every other stroke.
    - The line across the lower part of the hand is clearly shorter than the long line across the shaft, about three quarters as long.
  strokes:
    - where, a short stroke at the top left, slanting down to the left
      part: the fingertips of the top fingers, bending over
    - where, a long line across the middle, rising slightly to the right, from the far left nearly to the right
      part: the halberd's long shaft, held across the hand
    - where, a line down the left half from its top, ending in a small hook up to the left
      part: the hand's middle finger running down through the palm, the hook where the wrist turns
    - where, a line across the lower left, going right and turning up a little, about three quarters as long as the shaft
      part: the thumb and little finger, spread wide
    - where, a long stroke from the top centre down the right side, bending to the lower right and ending in a hook up
      part: the halberd's long curved pole with its blade end
    - where, a sweep from the middle right, crossing the pole, down and out to the bottom centre
      part: the halberd's side blade, swinging down to the left
    - where, a very short dot-like stroke at the top, slanting down to the right
      part: the small tassel tied at the halberd's top

言:
  object: a mouth with a tongue sticking out of it
  history: >-
    An ideogram: a tongue sticking out of a mouth 口. The oldest forms drew the mouth below and a
    tongue shape above it.
  layout: >-
    A wide open mouth sits in the lower half, seen from the front. A pale pink tongue sticks out of it
    upward and fills the upper half, wide at its base. The mouth is pale red with a pale lip line. Nothing
    else is in the picture.
  exact:
    - The mouth is a small closed square, its three sides joined; its left side and its top are joined at the top left corner.
    - The top of the mouth ends in a small hook down to the left at its right side, and touches the bottom line at the right.
    - The long line across the top is the longest stroke; it is more than twice as long as each of the two short bars below it.
    - The two short bars between the tongue and the mouth are about the same length, each about a third as long as the long line across the top.
    - The dot above the long line does not touch it; a small gap lies between them.
    - The bottom line of the mouth is about as long as each short bar, and clearly shorter than the top of the mouth.
  strokes:
    - where, a short dot at the top centre, slanting down to the right, above the long line and not touching it
      part: the tip of the tongue
    - where, a long line across the upper part, from near the left edge to near the right edge, a little higher on the right
      part: the tongue's broad base, laid across
    - where, a short bar across the upper middle, a third as long as the long line
      part: the upper fold of the tongue
    - where, a short bar across below it, about the same length
      part: the lower fold of the tongue
    - where, a short line down the left side of the lower part
      part: the mouth's left lip
    - where, a line across the top of the lower part, turning down at the right and ending in a small hook to the left
      part: the mouth's upper lip and its right corner
    - where, a short line across the bottom of the lower part, rising a little to the right
      part: the mouth's lower lip

間:
  object: the sun shining through a doorway of two door leaves standing ajar
  history: >-
    An ideogram: the sun 日 seen through the gap of a doorway 門. The oldest forms drew the moon
    shining through the gap; the later form put the sun in it.
  layout: >-
    Two tall pale wooden door leaves stand side by side with a gap between them, under a crossbeam at the
    top; the doorway is open at the bottom. Through the gap, in the lower middle, a small round pale sun
    shines with soft light. The left leaf is a little taller than the right one reads, the right leaf is
    wider at the top.
  exact:
    - The left leaf's inner edge (the short stroke across and down at the top left) starts at the top of the left post and stops well above the sun.
    - The two small dots beside the left leaf's inner edge are marks on the leaf and do not touch the inner edge; the lower one meets the end of the inner edge.
    - The right leaf's top and right side are one stroke, across and turning down, ending in a small hook to the left at the bottom.
    - The right leaf's short inner stroke hangs from the start of that top stroke; the lowest of its two small marks meets its end.
    - The long left post is the longest stroke; the short inner strokes are about two fifths as long.
    - The sun is a square-ish sun with a short bar in its middle that touches the left side and stops short of the right side.
    - The sun's top and right side are one stroke turning down and ending in a small hook to the left; the sun's bottom bar meets the end of that stroke.
    - The sun's left side and its top start at the same point.
    - The sun lies completely in the gap and touches no door stroke.
  strokes:
    - where, a long straight line down the left side, from near the top to near the bottom
      part: the left door leaf's outer post
    - where, a short line across the top left, starting at the top of the left post and turning down
      part: the left leaf's lintel and its inner edge
    - where, a small dot beside the inner edge, slanting up to the right, near its upper part
      part: a small panel mark on the left leaf
    - where, a small dot below it, slanting up to the right, where the inner edge ends
      part: a lower panel mark on the left leaf
    - where, a short line down from the top centre
      part: the right leaf's inner edge
    - where, a long line across the top of the right half, turning down at the right and ending in a small hook up to the left at the bottom
      part: the right leaf's lintel and its outer post
    - where, a small dot between the inner edge and the outer post, rising slightly to the right
      part: a small panel mark on the right leaf
    - where, a short line across below it, rising slightly to the right, ending on the outer post
      part: the right leaf's cross rail
    - where, a line down the left side of the sun
      part: the sun's left rim
    - where, a line across the top of the sun, turning down at the top right to run down the right side and ending in a small hook to the left
      part: the sun's top and right rim
    - where, a short line across the sun's middle, touching the left rim and stopping short of the right
      part: the bright band across the sun's heart
    - where, a short line across the sun's bottom, rising slightly to the right
      part: the sun's bottom rim

间:
  object: the sun shining through a doorway of two door leaves standing ajar
  history: >-
    An ideogram: the sun 日 seen through the gap of a doorway 门. The oldest forms drew the moon
    shining through the gap; the later form put the sun in it.
  layout: >-
    A tall pale wooden gateway: a left post and a crossbeam turning down into the right post, with an
    iron latch at the top left. In the open space inside, in the middle, a small round pale sun
    shines with soft light. The gateway is open at the bottom, and the sun floats well inside it, touching nothing.
  exact:
    - The latch is a short dot at the top left and stands free; it does not touch the post or the crossbeam.
    - The long left post is the longest stroke and stands free of the crossbeam; a small gap lies between its top and the crossbeam's start.
    - The crossbeam and the right post are one stroke, across and turning down, ending in a small hook to the left at the bottom.
    - The sun is a square-ish sun, its left side and its top start at the same point.
    - The sun's short middle bar touches the left side and stops short of the right side, and is clearly shorter than the bottom bar.
    - The sun's top and right side are one stroke turning down; the bottom bar ends on the right side.
    - The sun is about a third as wide as the gateway and lies completely inside.
  strokes:
    - where, a short dot at the top left, slanting down to the right
      part: an iron latch at the top of the left post
    - where, a long straight line down the left side
      part: the left gatepost
    - where, a long line across the top, turning down at the top right and running down the right side, ending in a small hook up to the left
      part: the crossbeam and the right gatepost
    - where, a line down the left side of the sun, in the middle of the gateway
      part: the sun's left rim
    - where, a line across the top of the sun, rising slightly to the right, then turning down to run down the right side
      part: the sun's top and right rim
    - where, a short line across the sun's middle, touching the left rim and stopping short of the right
      part: the bright band across the sun's heart
    - where, a slightly longer line across the sun's bottom, rising slightly to the right, ending on the right rim
      part: the sun's bottom rim

來:
  object: a wheat plant that has just borne fruit, its grain ears hanging on both sides of the stalk
  history: >-
    The oldest forms drew a wheat plant with its leaves and roots. Later the plant was drawn with a tree-like stalk,
    and the grain ears became short strokes at its sides.
  layout: >-
    One tall pale golden wheat stalk fills the square from the top to the bottom, with a short pair of leaves near its
    top and two roots spreading out at the bottom. Below the leaves, a small bunch of grain hangs on each side of the
    stalk, like two small ears. The left ear is a curved stroke with a grain beside it, the right ear the same.
    Colours are pale gold and pale green.
  exact:
    - The line across at the top is short and rises a little to the right; the stalk crosses it near its start.
    - The stalk is a straight vertical line ending in a plain point, with no hook.
    - The two roots start close together on the stalk, below the middle, and do not touch each other; one sweeps to the bottom left, the other to the bottom right and ends turning across.
    - Each ear is a curved stroke with a small dot beside it; each dot touches its ear's stroke.
    - The ears stand free of the stalk and of the line across, small gaps lie between them.
    - The right root is a little longer than the left root; both are about half as long as the stalk.
  strokes:
    - where, a short line across the top, rising slightly to the right, crossing the stalk near its start
      part: the plant's short top leaves
    - where, a stroke on the left of the stalk, running down and curving to the lower left
      part: the left ear of grain, hanging down
    - where, a small dot beside the left ear, slanting down to the right
      part: a single grain of the left ear
    - where, a stroke on the right of the stalk, running down and curving to the lower left
      part: the right ear of grain, hanging down
    - where, a small dot beside the right ear, slanting down to the right
      part: a single grain of the right ear
    - where, a long straight line down the middle, from the top to the bottom
      part: the wheat stalk
    - where, a sweep from the middle of the stalk down and out to the bottom left
      part: the root spreading to the left
    - where, a sweep from the middle of the stalk down and out to the bottom right, turning across at its end
      part: the root spreading to the right

来:
  object: a wheat plant that has just borne fruit, its grain ears hanging on both sides of the stalk
  history: >-
    The oldest forms drew a wheat plant with its leaves and roots. Later the plant was drawn with a tree-like stalk,
    and the grain ears became short strokes at its sides.
  layout: >-
    One tall pale golden wheat stalk fills the square from the top to the bottom, with a short pair of leaves near its
    top, a long pair of leaves a little above the middle, and two roots spreading out at the bottom. Between the two
    pairs of leaves, a small grain hangs on each side of the stalk. Colours are pale gold and pale green.
  exact:
    - The short line across at the top is clearly shorter than the long line across below it, a little over half as long.
    - The long line across the middle is the widest stroke and crosses the stalk; the short one also crosses it.
    - The stalk is a straight vertical line ending in a plain point, with no hook.
    - The two small grains lie between the two lines across and touch neither of them nor the stalk.
    - The left grain is a short dot slanting down to the right; the right grain is slightly longer and curls down to the left at its end.
    - The two roots start together on the stalk at the long line across and do not touch each other; the right root is a little longer and ends turning across.
  strokes:
    - where, a short line across the upper part, rising slightly to the right, crossing the stalk
      part: the plant's short top leaves
    - where, a short dot-like stroke left of the stalk, slanting down to the right
      part: a grain of wheat on the left
    - where, a short stroke right of the stalk, slanting down to the right and curling back down to the left
      part: a grain of wheat on the right
    - where, a long line across the middle, rising slightly to the right, crossing the stalk and reaching almost both sides
      part: the long pair of leaves, spreading wide
    - where, a long straight line down the middle, from the top to the bottom
      part: the wheat stalk
    - where, a sweep from the stalk at the long line across, down and out to the bottom left
      part: the root spreading to the left
    - where, a longer sweep from the stalk at the long line across, down and out to the bottom right, turning across at its end
      part: the root spreading to the right

你:
  object: a person on the left facing right, addressing the other person standing on the right, the one called you
  history: >-
    An ideogram: the pronoun 尔 for a person 亻. It names the person one is speaking to.
  layout: >-
    The person on the left fills the left third and faces right, toward the other. The other person fills the
    right two thirds, seen from the front, standing with legs apart and one arm held out to the right.
    Both have friendly faces with eyes, a nose and a mouth, and wear simple pale clothes.
  exact:
    - The left person's second stroke starts on the first stroke, in its middle, and the two do not cross.
    - The right person's arm line starts on the first sweep, rising slightly to the right, and ends in a small hook down to the left.
    - The right person's body is a long vertical ending in a small hook up to the left; it does not touch the arm line, a small gap lies between them.
    - The two short strokes at the lower part are the legs; the left leg is a short vertical, the right leg a short slant down to the right; they do not touch the body or each other.
    - The two people do not touch each other.
    - The left person is taller than the right person's legs but about as tall as the right person's whole figure.
  strokes:
    - where, a stroke in the left third from the top down, turning to sweep down to the bottom left
      part: the left person's head, back and left leg
    - where, a vertical line in the left third starting on the middle of the first stroke and going down
      part: the left person's right leg
    - where, a sweep at the top of the right part, from the top centre down to the left
      part: the right person's head and shoulder
    - where, a line across the upper middle of the right part, rising slightly to the right and ending in a small hook down to the left
      part: the right person's arm, held out to the right with the hand hooked
    - where, a long line down the middle of the right part, ending in a small hook up to the left
      part: the right person's body, the hook the foot
    - where, a short vertical in the lower left of the right part, left of the body
      part: the right person's left leg
    - where, a short stroke in the lower right of the right part, slanting down to the right
      part: the right person's right leg

作:
  object: a person on the left building a simple wooden frame on the right for the first time
  history: >-
    An ideogram: a person 亻 making something for the first time 乍. The right part also gives the sound.
  layout: >-
    The person fills the left third, faces right and reaches toward the frame, with a friendly face and
    simple pale clothes. The frame fills the right two thirds: a new pale wooden frame that stands on its own,
    with an upright post, a beam across its top and two rails across its post. Pale wood colours.
  exact:
    - The person's second stroke starts on the middle of the first stroke and does not touch the frame.
    - The frame's leaning plank starts at the top and does not touch the upright post; a small gap lies between them.
    - The frame's top beam starts on the leaning plank and rises to the right, and the post hangs from its start.
    - The upright post is the longest stroke of the frame and ends in a plain point at the bottom, with no hook.
    - The two rails are the same length, each about half as long as the top beam, and both start on the post.
  strokes:
    - where, a stroke in the left third from the top down, turning to sweep down to the bottom left
      part: the person's head, back and left leg
    - where, a vertical line in the left third starting on the middle of the first stroke and going down
      part: the person's right leg
    - where, a stroke in the right part from the top centre down to the left, ending in the middle
      part: the frame's leaning plank
    - where, a line across the middle of the right part, rising to the right, starting on the plank
      part: the frame's top beam
    - where, a long vertical line down from the start of the beam to the bottom
      part: the frame's upright post
    - where, a short line across the post, rising slightly to the right, half as long as the beam
      part: the frame's upper rail
    - where, a short line across the post lower down, rising slightly to the right, the same length
      part: the frame's lower rail

还:
  object: a bird flying up into the sky above a road, the road bending at the left
  history: >-
    An ideogram: not 不 moving 辶. The right part is a bird flying upward; the walking radical is the road.
  layout: >-
    The bird flies upward in the upper right, pale blue and grey, wings spread. The road runs along the
    bottom and up the left side: a pale road that bends at the left, with a small pale roadside stone at the
    top left. Nothing else is in the picture.
  exact:
    - The sky line across the top starts at the left of the bird and the left wing starts at its middle.
    - The bird's body starts on the left wing, a little below the top, and runs down bending slightly to the right.
    - The bird's right wing is a short stroke slanting down to the right and stands free of every other stroke.
    - The roadside stone is a short dot at the top left and stands free of every other stroke.
    - The road's bend and the long road are drawn as two strokes; the bend ends on the long road and the long road runs below everything else, rising a little at its end.
    - The sky line is about half as long as the long road.
    - The bird's body stops short of the road; a small gap lies between them.
  strokes:
    - where, a long line across the top of the right part, rising slightly to the right
      part: the line of the sky
    - where, a long sweep starting at the middle of the sky line, running down and out to the bottom left
      part: the bird's left wing
    - where, a stroke starting on the left wing, a little below the top, running down and bending slightly to the right
      part: the bird's body and tail
    - where, a short stroke right of the body, slanting down to the right, standing free
      part: the bird's right wing
    - where, a short dot at the top left, slanting down to the right
      part: a small roadside stone
    - where, a stroke from the middle of the left side, rising a little to the right and then turning down to the lower left
      part: the road's bend
    - where, a long line along the bottom, from the left edge to the right edge, rising slightly toward its end
      part: the long road

私:
  object: a stalk of grain on the left and a small private enclosure on the right, kept to oneself
  history: >-
    The right part 厶 gives both the meaning and the sound. The oldest forms drew it as a closed curl for what
    is one's own.
  layout: >-
    A pale golden stalk of grain fills the left half, its ear drooping over to the left. On the right, a small
    pale curved fence closes in a little plot of ground, a private patch, with a short gate post at its
    right. Colours are pale.
  exact:
    - The ear stroke at the top is short and curls over to the left; the stalk starts on its middle.
    - The stalk is a straight vertical line ending in a plain point, with no hook.
    - The line across the stalk crosses it a little above the middle and reaches both sides.
    - The left root is a long sweep to the bottom left; the right root is a short dot, only about a third as long as the left root.
    - The enclosure's wall turns across at the bottom and rises slightly to the right; it does not touch the short gate post at its start, but the end touches it.
    - The enclosure does not touch the grain.
  strokes:
    - where, a short stroke at the top, starting at the top centre and curling over to the left
      part: the ear of grain, drooping over to the left
    - where, a line across the left half, a little above the middle, rising slightly to the right
      part: the leaves, spreading to both sides
    - where, a long straight line down the middle of the left half, from near the top to the bottom
      part: the stalk
    - where, a sweep from the stalk down and out to the bottom left
      part: the root spreading to the left
    - where, a short dot-like stroke from the stalk slanting down to the right
      part: the short root on the right
    - where, a long stroke down the right half from the top, turning at the bottom to run across, rising slightly to the right
      part: the enclosure's curved wall
    - where, a short stroke inside the curve, slanting down to the right and ending on the wall
      part: the enclosure's short gate post

裡:
  object: a farm field above a mound of earth, the land measured as a village unit, and a small robe on the left
  history: >-
    An ideogram: farm 田 land 土 as a unit of measure. The robe 衤 gives only the sound.
  layout: >-
    On the right, a square farm field seen from above, divided by raised banks into plots, lies above a pale
    mound of earth with a flat ground line below it. A boundary stake runs down through the field and into the
    earth. On the left a small plain robe hangs, seen from the front, in pale colours.
  exact:
    - The robe's dot at the top is short and stands free; it does not touch the sleeve line.
    - The robe's sleeve line runs across, rising slightly to the right, and turns down to the left at its end.
    - The robe's inner panel edge starts on the middle of the sleeve line, and the dot to its right and the short stroke below the dot do not touch it.
    - The field's left side stops short of its middle bar, a small gap lies between them; its end meets the start of the bottom bar.
    - The field's top and right side are one stroke, across and turning down; the field's short bottom bar rises to the right and ends on that right side.
    - The field's short middle bar is crossed by the boundary stake.
    - The boundary stake starts at the top left of the field's top stroke, crosses the middle bar and the bottom bar, and the earth's short line across, and ends on the ground line.
    - The earth's short line across is a little longer than the field's middle bar, but about half as long as the ground line.
    - The ground line is the longest stroke on the right, nearly its full width.
  strokes:
    - where, a short dot at the top of the left part, slanting down to the right
      part: the robe's collar
    - where, a long line across the left part, rising slightly to the right and turning down to the left at its end
      part: the robe's sleeves, then the left lapel falling down
    - where, a short line down left of centre in the left part
      part: the edge of the inner panel
    - where, a short dot at the right of the left part, slanting down to the left
      part: the fold of the right lapel
    - where, a short stroke below the dot, slanting down to the right
      part: the robe's hem on the right
    - where, a line down the left side of the field
      part: the bank along the field's left edge
    - where, a line across the top of the field, turning down at the right and running down the right side
      part: the banks along the top and the right edge
    - where, a short line across the middle of the field
      part: the bank dividing the upper plots from the lower
    - where, a short stroke across the bottom of the field, rising to the right and ending on the right side
      part: the bank along the bottom edge
    - where, a long line down from the top left of the field's top stroke to the ground line
      part: the boundary stake through the field and the earth
    - where, a short line across the lower part, crossing the stake, rising slightly to the right
      part: the top ridge of the mound of earth
    - where, a long line across the bottom, nearly the full width of the right part
      part: the ground line under the mound

里:
  object: a farm field above a mound of earth, the land measured as a village unit
  history: >-
    An ideogram: farm 田 land 土 as a unit of measure.
  layout: >-
    A square farm field seen from above, divided by raised banks into plots, lies at the top. Below it
    sits a pale mound of earth on a flat ground line. A boundary stake runs down the middle, through the
    field and into the earth. Colours are pale greens and browns.
  exact:
    - The field's left side turns down and its end meets the start of the bottom bar.
    - The field's top and right side are one stroke, across and turning down, nearly as long as the whole field is wide.
    - The field's middle bar is short and does not touch the field's left side or its right side; it is crossed by the boundary stake.
    - The field's bottom bar is short, a little longer than the middle bar, rising to the right and ending on the right side.
    - The boundary stake starts on the middle of the field's top stroke, runs the whole height of the field and the earth, and ends on the ground line.
    - The earth's short line across crosses the stake and is a little under half as long as the ground line.
    - The ground line is the longest stroke across, nearly the full width.
  strokes:
    - where, a line down the left side of the field, slanting a little to the right, then turning down
      part: the bank along the field's left edge
    - where, a line across the top of the field, turning down at the right and running down the right side
      part: the banks along the top and the right edge
    - where, a short line across the middle of the field, rising slightly to the right
      part: the bank dividing the upper plots from the lower
    - where, a short line across the bottom of the field, rising slightly to the right, ending on the right side
      part: the bank along the bottom edge
    - where, a long line down the middle from the field's top stroke to the ground line
      part: the boundary stake through the field and into the earth
    - where, a short line across the lower part, crossing the stake
      part: the top ridge of the mound of earth
    - where, a long line across the bottom, nearly the full width
      part: the ground line under the mound

社:
  object: an altar of the god of the soil on the left, beside a mound of earth on the right
  history: >-
    An ideogram: an earth 土 spirit 礻. The earth part also gives the sound. The oldest forms drew a mound
    of earth for the soil god.
  layout: >-
    A stone altar fills the left half: an offering slab above a table on a single post, with two small braces.
    On the right a pale mound of earth stands on a flat ground line, with a short ridge across it. Pale
    stone grey and earth brown colours.
  exact:
    - The offering slab above the altar is a short dot and stands free of every other stroke.
    - The altar's table top is the longest stroke on the left; it runs across, rising a little to the right, and turns down to the left at its end.
    - The altar's post starts on the middle of the table top and does not touch the left brace's dot; a small gap lies between them.
    - The left brace is a dot slanting down to the right and stands free.
    - The altar's post stops short of the ground line; a small gap lies between its middle and the line's start.
    - The mound's short line across is crossed by the stake and is about half as long as the ground line.
    - The stake runs from near the top to the ground line, down to the right and then turning down, and touches the middle of the ground line.
    - The ground line is the longest stroke on the right, a little over half as long as the table top.
  strokes:
    - where, a short stroke at the top of the left half, slanting down to the right
      part: the offering slab above the altar
    - where, a long line across the middle of the left half, rising a little to the right and turning down to the left at its end
      part: the altar's table top
    - where, a line down from the middle of the table, left of centre
      part: the post that holds the table
    - where, a short dot at the lower right of the left half, slanting down to the right
      part: the brace
    - where, a short line across the middle of the right half, rising slightly to the right
      part: the top ridge of the mound of earth
    - where, a long stroke from the top of the right half, slanting a little to the right and turning down to the ground line
      part: the stake driven through the mound
    - where, a long line across the bottom of the right half
      part: the ground line under the mound

初:
  object: a piece of cloth on the left being cut by a knife on the right, the first step in making clothes
  history: >-
    An ideogram: cutting with a knife 刀 the cloth 衤, the first step in making clothes.
  layout: >-
    On the left a plain pale robe cloth hangs, seen from the front. On the right a large pale grey knife
    stands upright, its blade pointing down and its handle at the top. Nothing else is in the picture.
  exact:
    - The cloth's dot at the top left stands free of every other stroke.
    - The cloth's sleeve line runs across, rising slightly to the right, and turns down to the left at its end; the inner panel edge starts on its middle.
    - The dot at the right of the cloth and the stroke below it do not touch the inner panel edge; the dot touches the stroke below it.
    - The knife's handle and back are one stroke, across and turning down, ending in a small hook up to the left at the bottom.
    - The knife's second stroke starts on the start of the first stroke and sweeps down to the left, shorter than the first.
    - The knife does not touch the cloth; the sleeve line stops short of the knife's top.
  strokes:
    - where, a short dot at the top of the left part, slanting down to the right
      part: the cloth's collar
    - where, a long line across the left part, rising slightly to the right and turning down to the left at its end
      part: the cloth's shoulders, then the left lapel falling down
    - where, a short line down left of centre in the left part
      part: the edge of the inner panel
    - where, a short dot at the right of the left part, slanting down to the left
      part: the fold of the right lapel
    - where, a short stroke below the dot, slanting down to the right
      part: the cloth's hem on the right
    - where, a long line across the top of the right part, turning down and ending in a small hook up to the left
      part: the knife's handle and its straight back
    - where, a sweep from the start of the first knife stroke, down and out to the bottom left
      part: the knife's blade edge, pointing down

車:
  object: an old two-wheeled cart seen from straight above, its axle running from top to bottom
  history: >-
    A pictograph of a cart with two wheels seen from above. The oldest forms drew the wheels, the axle and the
    box between them; later forms straightened them into lines.
  layout: >-
    The cart fills the square, seen from straight above, pale wood and pale grey. A short crossbar lies near the top,
    the cart's box sits in the middle and a long line for the wheels lies lower, all joined by a straight axle
    from the top to the bottom. The box is open and holds nothing.
  exact:
    - The short line across the top is crossed by the axle near its start, and is clearly shorter than the wheel line, a little under half as long.
    - The long line across the lower part is the longest stroke; it is crossed by the axle.
    - The box's first stroke slants down to the right; the box's top and right side are one stroke, across and turning down, about four fifths as long as the wheel line.
    - The box's left side and the stroke that follows it stop short of the box's middle bar; small gaps lie between them.
    - The box's middle bar is short, about a quarter of the wheel line, and the box's bottom bar is a little longer, both rising slightly to the right.
    - The box's right side ends where the bottom bar ends.
    - The box stops short of the wheel line; small gaps lie between them.
    - The axle crosses the box, the middle bar, the bottom bar and the wheel line.
  strokes:
    - where, a short line across the top, rising slightly to the right, crossed by the axle near its start
      part: the near wheel, seen edge on, short
    - where, a short stroke at the box's left side, slanting down to the right
      part: the left side of the cart's box
    - where, a line across the top of the box, turning down at the right and running down the right side
      part: the cart box's front and right side
    - where, a short line across the box's middle, rising slightly to the right
      part: a slat across the box
    - where, a slightly longer line across the box's bottom, rising slightly to the right
      part: the box's bottom edge
    - where, a long line across the lower part, nearly the full width
      part: the far wheel, seen edge on, long
    - where, a long straight line down the middle from near the top to the bottom, crossing every line across
      part: the axle and the shaft

連:
  object: an old two-wheeled cart seen from straight above, its axle running from top to bottom, rolling along a road
  history: >-
    An ideogram: a cart moving along a road, carrying goods between cities. The oldest forms drew the cart
    and the road.
  layout: >-
    The cart fills the right three quarters of the square, seen from straight above, pale wood and pale grey, with
    a few pale sacks of goods in its box. The road is pale tan: it curves up on the left and runs along the bottom
    beneath the cart. Nothing else is in the picture.
  exact:
    - The short line across the top is crossed by the axle near its start, and is clearly shorter than the road, a little over a third as long.
    - The box's first stroke is a vertical, and the box's top and right side are one stroke, across rising slightly to the right and turning down.
    - The box's middle bar is short and the box's bottom bar is a short stroke rising to the right; its start meets the end of the first vertical.
    - The wheel line near the bottom is about half as long as the road, a little under, and stops short of the box; a small gap lies between them.
    - The axle is crossed by the top line, the box's top, the middle bar, the bottom bar and the wheel line.
    - The axle stops just short of the road; a small gap lies between them.
    - The road's dot at the top left stands free.
    - The road's bend ends on the long road line, and the long road line runs below everything else, rising a little at its end.
  strokes:
    - where, a short line across the top of the cart, rising slightly to the right, crossed by the axle near its start
      part: the near wheel, seen edge on
    - where, a short vertical line at the left of the box
      part: the left side of the cart's box
    - where, a line across the top of the box, rising slightly to the right and turning down at the right
      part: the cart box's front and right side
    - where, a short line across the box's middle, rising slightly to the right
      part: a slat across the box
    - where, a short stroke across the box's bottom, rising to the right
      part: the box's bottom edge
    - where, a line across the lower part, about half as long as the road
      part: the far wheel, seen edge on
    - where, a long straight line down the middle, from the top to near the bottom, crossing every line across
      part: the axle and the shaft
    - where, a short dot at the top left, slanting down to the right, standing free
      part: a small stone at the roadside
    - where, a stroke from the middle of the left side, rising a little to the right and turning down to the lower left
      part: the road's bend
    - where, a long line along the bottom from the left edge to the right edge, rising slightly toward its end
      part: the long road

连:
  object: an old two-wheeled cart seen from straight above, its axle running from top to bottom, rolling along a road
  history: >-
    An ideogram: a cart moving along a road, carrying goods between cities. The oldest forms drew the cart
    and the road.
  layout: >-
    The cart fills the right three quarters of the square, seen from straight above, pale wood and pale grey, with
    a few pale sacks of goods in its box. The road is pale tan: it curves up on the left and runs along the bottom
    beneath the cart. Nothing else is in the picture.
  exact:
    - The line across the upper part is short, a little under half as long as the road, and is crossed by the side-of-the-box stroke.
    - The side-of-the-box stroke starts above that line, runs down, and turns across to the right at the middle.
    - The long line across the lower part is a little over half as long as the road.
    - The axle starts below the upper line, does not touch it, crosses the box's turning stroke and the long lower line, and stops just short of the road.
    - The road's dot at the top left stands free.
    - The road's bend ends on the long road line, and the long road line runs below everything else, rising a little at its end.
  strokes:
    - where, a short line across the upper part, rising slightly to the right
      part: the near wheel, seen edge on
    - where, a stroke from the top centre down, turning across to the right in the middle
      part: the side and front of the cart's box
    - where, a long line across the lower part
      part: the far wheel, seen edge on
    - where, a straight line down the centre from just below the top line to near the bottom
      part: the axle and the shaft
    - where, a short dot at the top left, slanting down to the right, standing free
      part: a small stone at the roadside
    - where, a stroke from the middle of the left side, rising a little to the right and turning down to the lower left
      part: the road's bend
    - where, a long line along the bottom from the left edge to the right edge, rising slightly toward its end
      part: the long road

報:
  object: a pair of manacles on the left, and on the right a kneeling person seized by a hand, being sentenced
  history: >-
    An ideogram: a hand 又 seizing a kneeling criminal 卩 and cuffing him with manacles 幸, a sentence being passed.
  layout: >-
    On the left stand pale grey manacles, a locking pin on top and two bands joined by a post. On the right a
    person kneels, seen from the side and facing right, with head bowed, a face with eyes, a nose and a mouth,
    and simple pale clothes. A hand grips the person's back from the right. Colours are pale.
  exact:
    - The manacles' locking pin on top is a cross, a short line across and a short stroke down through it.
    - The pin's stroke down ends on the top band and crosses nothing else.
    - The top band is long, rising to the right; it stops short of the kneeling person, a small gap lies between them.
    - The dot and the short stroke below the top band, and the short bar under them, are the rivets and the middle band.
    - The lower band rises more steeply to the right and is crossed by the manacles' post.
    - The post goes down from the middle band, crosses the lower band and ends in a plain point.
    - The person's back is the longest vertical of the right part; the head stroke starts at its top.
    - The head stroke turns down and ends in a small hook to the left.
    - The hand's first stroke starts on the back, runs across, turns down and then down to the left.
    - The hand's second stroke starts on the back, slants down to the right and ends turning across to the right.
  strokes:
    - where, a short line across the top left, rising slightly to the right
      part: the manacles' locking pin, its bar
    - where, a short stroke down through the bar, slanting down to the right
      part: the manacles' locking pin, its stem
    - where, a line across the left half, rising slightly to the right, about as long as the left half is wide
      part: the manacles' top band
    - where, a small dot below the top band, slanting down to the right
      part: the left rivet
    - where, a short stroke down below the top band
      part: the right rivet
    - where, a short line across below it, rising slightly to the right
      part: the manacles' middle band
    - where, a long line across the lower part, rising to the right
      part: the manacles' lower band
    - where, a vertical line down from the middle band to the bottom, crossing the lower band
      part: the manacles' post
    - where, a line across the top of the right part, rising slightly to the right, then turning down and ending in a small hook to the left
      part: the kneeling person's bowed head and raised arm
    - where, a long line down the left edge of the right part
      part: the kneeling person's back and legs
    - where, a line across the middle of the right part, turning down and then down to the left
      part: the hand's back and curled fingers
    - where, a stroke from the back, slanting down to the right and ending across to the right
      part: the hand's arm reaching in from the lower right

报:
  object: a hand on the left, and on the right a kneeling person seized by a second hand, being sentenced
  history: >-
    An ideogram: a hand 扌 seizing a kneeling criminal 卩, with a hand 又 cuffing him, a sentence being passed.
  layout: >-
    On the left an open hand, palm toward the viewer with the wrist below, reaches up. On the right a person
    kneels, seen from the side and facing right, with head bowed, a face with eyes, a nose and a mouth, and
    simple pale clothes. A second hand grips the person's back from the right. Colours are pale.
  exact:
    - The hand's vertical is the longest stroke, ending in a small hook up to the left, and is crossed by the line across and by the rising line.
    - The hand's line across is about half as long as the vertical's height, and the rising line is a little longer.
    - The kneeling person's head stroke starts at the top of the back and turns down, ending in a small hook up to the left.
    - The hand at the person's back starts on the back, and its two strokes cross each other.
    - The hand's second stroke slants down to the right and turns across at its end.
    - The line across of the left hand stops short of the person's back; a gap lies between them.
  strokes:
    - where, a short line across the upper left, rising slightly to the right
      part: the fingers spreading out to both sides
    - where, a long line down the left part from the top, ending in a small hook up to the left
      part: the middle finger running down through the palm, the hook where the wrist turns
    - where, a line across the lower left part, rising to the right
      part: the thumb and little finger, spread wide
    - where, a line across the top of the right part, turning down and ending in a small hook up to the left
      part: the kneeling person's bowed head and raised arm
    - where, a long line down the left edge of the right part
      part: the kneeling person's back and legs
    - where, a line across the middle of the right part, rising slightly to the right, turning down and then down to the left
      part: the second hand's back and curled fingers
    - where, a stroke from the back, slanting down to the right and ending across to the right
      part: the second hand's arm reaching in from the lower right

位:
  object: a person standing next to another person standing upright on the ground, the place where a person stands
  history: >-
    An ideogram: the place where a person 亻 stands 立. The right part draws a person standing on the ground.
  layout: >-
    The person on the left fills the left third and faces right. The second person stands on the ground line at
    the right, facing you, arms held out. Both have friendly faces with eyes, a nose and a mouth, and wear
    simple pale clothes. The ground is a pale green line under the second person only.
  exact:
    - The left person's second stroke starts on the middle of the first stroke and does not reach the ground line; a small gap lies between them.
    - The second person's head is a short dot at the top and stands free.
    - The arms line is the longest stroke above the legs, about two thirds as long as the ground line, and stands free.
    - The right leg starts below the arms, runs down to the left and ends on the ground line.
    - The left leg is a short dot and stands free of the ground line.
    - The right leg is clearly longer than the left leg, more than twice as long.
    - The ground line is the longest stroke across, about as long as the second person's width.
  strokes:
    - where, a stroke in the left third from the top down, turning to sweep down to the bottom left
      part: the left person's head, back and left leg
    - where, a vertical line in the left third starting on the middle of the first stroke and going down
      part: the left person's right leg
    - where, a short dot at the top centre of the right part, slanting down to the right
      part: the second person's head
    - where, a line across, a little above the middle of the right part, rising slightly to the right
      part: the arms, held out to both sides
    - where, a short stroke at the lower left of the arms, slanting down to the right
      part: the left leg
    - where, a stroke at the lower right, slanting down to the right and then turning down to the left to the ground line
      part: the right leg, standing on the ground
    - where, a long line across the bottom of the right part
      part: the ground

利:
  object: a stalk of grain on the left and a knife on the right, harvesting
  history: >-
    An ideogram: harvesting grain 禾 with a knife 刂. The oldest forms drew the knife cutting grain.
  layout: >-
    A pale golden stalk of grain fills the left half, its ear drooping over to the left. On the right a long
    pale grey knife stands upright beside it, the handle at the top and the blade pointing down.
    Colours are pale.
  exact:
    - The ear stroke at the top is short and curls over to the left; the stalk starts on its middle.
    - The stalk is a straight vertical line ending in a plain point, with no hook.
    - The line across the stalk crosses it a little above the middle and reaches both sides.
    - The left root is a long sweep to the bottom left; the right root is a short dot, only about a third as long as the left root.
    - The knife's long stroke ends in a small hook up to the left; its short stroke beside it is only about a third as long.
    - The knife does not touch the grain, and the two knife strokes do not touch each other.
  strokes:
    - where, a short stroke at the top, starting at the top centre and curling over to the left
      part: the ear of grain, drooping over to the left
    - where, a line across the left half, a little above the middle, rising slightly to the right
      part: the leaves, spreading to both sides
    - where, a long straight line down the middle of the left half, from near the top to the bottom
      part: the stalk
    - where, a sweep from the stalk down and out to the bottom left
      part: the root spreading to the left
    - where, a short dot-like stroke from the stalk slanting down to the right
      part: the short root on the right
    - where, a short vertical at the upper part of the right half, a third as long as the knife
      part: the knife's handle
    - where, a long line down the right half from the top, ending in a small hook up to the left
      part: the knife's blade, the hook its curled tip

身:
  object: a pregnant woman seen from the side, facing right, her belly rounded
  history: >-
    A pictograph of a pregnant woman. The oldest forms drew the rounded belly in profile.
  layout: >-
    The woman fills the square from head to feet, seen from the side, facing right, with a friendly face
    (eyes, a nose, a mouth) and a simple pale long dress. Her round belly bulges toward the right in the
    middle. Her hands rest on it. Colours are pale.
  exact:
    - The head stroke at the top bends down to the left and touches the start of the back; the back and the belly's front are joined at their start.
    - The belly's front is the longest stroke, it runs across to the right, turns down and ends in a small hook to the left at the bottom.
    - The two short strokes inside the belly are about the same length, each about a seventh as long as the belly's front, and they do not touch the front.
    - The lower curve of the belly starts on the left, runs down and then rises to the right, touching the belly's front.
    - The long skirt stroke runs from the upper right down to the bottom left and crosses the belly's front and ends at the left.
    - The back stroke slants down to the right and ends on the lower curve of the belly.
  strokes:
    - where, a short stroke at the top, slanting down to the right and then down to the left
      part: the woman's head, bowed a little
    - where, a stroke down the left side of the middle, slanting a little to the right
      part: the woman's back
    - where, a long stroke from the top, across to the right, turning down and ending in a small hook to the left at the bottom
      part: the front of her rounded belly, down to her foot
    - where, a short bar inside the belly, rising slightly to the right, high
      part: the upper fold of her dress over the belly
    - where, a short bar below it, rising slightly to the right
      part: the lower fold of her dress over the belly
    - where, a long stroke across the lower middle, going down to the right and rising at its end
      part: the lower curve of her belly
    - where, a long sweep from the upper right down to the bottom left
      part: the long skirt falling to the ground

两:
  object: two people sitting together in a cart under its canopy, holding the bar above
  history: >-
    An ideogram: two people 从 together in a cart. The oldest forms drew two matching things side by side.
  layout: >-
    A flat bar lies at the top across the picture. Below it hangs a pale cloth canopy, open at the bottom,
    with a post on its left, and under it two friendly people sit side by side, facing right, both with
    faces (eyes, a nose, a mouth) and simple pale clothes. They hold the bar. Colours are pale.
  exact:
    - The bar across the top is a plain flat line, a little under half as long as the canopy's top, and stands free of the canopy.
    - The canopy's top runs across, rising slightly to the right, and turns down at the right, ending in a small hook up to the left at the bottom.
    - The canopy's top is the longest stroke, longer than the bar by more than twice.
    - The canopy's left post starts at the canopy's top and is slightly more than a third as long as the whole.
    - The people's bodies start on the bar. The left person's body is a little shorter than the right person's.
    - The left person's small stroke touches the middle of the body; the right person's small stroke too.
    - The left person's body stops short of the left post; a small gap lies between them.
    - The right person's small stroke stops short of the canopy's top.
  strokes:
    - where, a short line across the top, a little under half as long as the canopy
      part: the bar the riders hold
    - where, a short line down the left side, slanting a little to the right
      part: the canopy's left post
    - where, a long line across the middle, rising slightly to the right, turning down at the right and ending in a small hook to the left
      part: the canopy's top and its right post
    - where, a stroke from the bar's middle, down and turning down to the left
      part: the left person's body, bowed
    - where, a short dot beside the left body, slanting down to the right
      part: the left person's front leg
    - where, a long stroke from the bar's middle, down and turning down to the left, longer than the left body
      part: the right person's body, bowed
    - where, a short stroke beside it, slanting down to the right
      part: the right person's front leg

兩:
  object: two people sitting together in a cart under its canopy, holding the bar above
  history: >-
    An ideogram: two people 入 together in a cart. The oldest forms drew two matching things side by side.
  layout: >-
    A flat bar lies at the top across the picture. Below it hangs a pale cloth canopy, open at the bottom,
    with a post on its left and a central pole, and under it two small friendly people sit side by side, facing
    the pole, both with faces (eyes, a nose, a mouth) and simple pale clothes. Colours are pale.
  exact:
    - The bar across the top is a plain flat line, about a third as long as the canopy's top, and stands free of the canopy.
    - The central pole hangs from the middle of the bar and crosses the canopy's top.
    - The canopy's top runs across and turns down at the right, ending in a small hook up to the left at the bottom; it is the longest stroke.
    - The canopy's left post starts at the canopy's top and is a little under half as long as the pole's reach.
    - The two people are small and equal in size, one on each side of the pole, and do not touch it.
    - Each person is a short slant down to the left that starts on the middle of a longer slant down to the right.
    - The left person's longer slant is about half as long as the right person's.
    - The people do not touch the left post or the canopy's top.
  strokes:
    - where, a short line across the top, about a third as long as the canopy
      part: the bar the riders hold
    - where, a short line down the left side, slanting a little to the right
      part: the canopy's left post
    - where, a long line across the middle, turning down at the right and ending in a small hook up to the left
      part: the canopy's top and its right post
    - where, a straight vertical line down the middle from the bar's middle to the bottom
      part: the canopy's central pole
    - where, a short stroke in the lower left, slanting down to the left
      part: the left person's back leg
    - where, a short stroke in the lower left, slanting down to the right, above the back leg's start
      part: the left person's body and front leg
    - where, a short stroke in the lower right, slanting down to the left
      part: the right person's back leg
    - where, a longer stroke in the lower right, slanting down to the right
      part: the right person's body and front leg
